WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
Jeff Deverett, Producer: Here’s the big&nbsp;
question, how does a new filmmaker even get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:08.480
the first appointment? How do you get the first&nbsp;
time in there? Here is how I think you get in.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:15.040
Film Courage: I believe you have&nbsp;
three movies currently on Netflix?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:17.760
Did you pitch all of those&nbsp;
movies yourself to Netflix?

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:23.600
Jeff: Okay so the three movies first of all FULL&nbsp;
OUT is the first movie (it’s a gymnastics movie).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.280
The second one is called KISS AND CRY (it’s a&nbsp;
skating movie). And the third one is called FALL&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:34.000
OUT 2: YOU GOT THIS (which is actually starting on&nbsp;
January 1st). So it’s already sold and delivered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:39.760
to Netflix but they’re actually starting airing&nbsp;
it on January 1st. It’s another gymnastics movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:44.160
we’re supposed to start it during the Olympics&nbsp;
last summer but of course there were no Olympics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:49.280
so they held it until January. I&nbsp;
say it’s on because it’s delivered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:55.920
and that’s when it’s going to start worldwide.&nbsp;
Luckily back then when I pitched the first one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:01.520
they were open to hear pitches from independent&nbsp;
producers. I got lucky because the timing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.560
They were a big company but they weren’t what&nbsp;
they are today. So they were still taking pitches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:14.560
from indie producers. So I went in there and I&nbsp;
pitched them myself and I learned a lot. It’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:19.520
whole different style. They get so many&nbsp;
pitches. I didn’t know this back then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.720
you’ve got to keep those pitches really short. Get&nbsp;
to the point because they get so many of them that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.680
they get tired of hearing them. They just want&nbsp;
to make a quick decision and hear what you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:35.600
to say. I pitched it too long. I had a 1/2 meeting&nbsp;
and I probably took 20 minutes to pitch that other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:42.720
than 10 minutes of pleasantries. Today I pitch in&nbsp;
2 minutes, 2 - 3 minutes max. That’s all it takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:49.040
because they generally know right? So I pitched&nbsp;
it and they said okay sounds good we like it let’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:56.000
do it but we don’t want to take delivery from&nbsp;
you because you’re an indie producer. We want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.040
to take delivery from a distribution company that&nbsp;
we’re already set up to do business with because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:07.160
we don’t want to set you up as a new client in&nbsp;
our database. It’s too much work for us. But I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:15.040
am also a distributor. I have my own distribution&nbsp;
company. I make delivery…I make perfect delivery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:19.920
to the distributors or I am making delivery to&nbsp;
the broadcasters around the world so delivery is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:25.200
not an issue. They said yeah but we’re so busy, we&nbsp;
have so much going on we would prefer that we put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:30.480
you through a distributor and I said I don’t have&nbsp;
a problem with that except for they’re going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:36.960
charge me a full fee for doing nothing. I pitched&nbsp;
you, I did the sale. You want the movie and I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:41.680
going to have to pay their full commission and&nbsp;
they said “Jeff you seem like a sharp guy. Figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:48.400
it out. Go negotiate it, figure it out.” So they&nbsp;
gave me a list of like 6 or 7 distributors that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:54.080
they recommended who I could do business with.&nbsp;
I met with 2 of them. I chose one of them and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.800 --> 00:03:01.520
cut a good deal but not a fantastic deal because&nbsp;
I had no leverage. They knew I was sent there so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:08.080
I had to deliver through another distributor and&nbsp;
I was kind of a little peeved by it. You know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:15.120
it was Netflix, that’s what they wanted. What&nbsp;
am I going to argue? So I had to do that deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:19.680
and it worked out well. The other people they&nbsp;
did the deal then they actually happen to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:23.200
a good distributor. They did some other deals&nbsp;
too and it worked out pretty well so I can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:29.920
complain about it. So the second time around same&nbsp;
thing - go to Netflix pitch them on the movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:35.040
Love it, let’s do it. I said guys&nbsp;
don’t make me go through a distributor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.800
They said Jeff you know the rules, got to go&nbsp;
through a distributor. I said how many times do we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:47.360
have to go down this anyways. Same thing happens.&nbsp;
I go and I use a different distributor because now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:52.560
it’s different times. I actually make a different&nbsp;
type of deal and it works up. Third time around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:04:02.240
let’s do it please let me just deliver to you.&nbsp;
Please let me deliver to you. If I fail listen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:07.920
don’t even pay me the licensing fee. If I don’t&nbsp;
deliver on time per exactly per your specs which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:12.880
I’m doing anyways to these other distributors I&nbsp;
said I’m already…they’re doing nothing. They’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:17.040
pushing a button to send the materials that I’ve&nbsp;
given them. I have your delivery list and your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:23.120
specs. I make the…I do all of the delivery items.&nbsp;
Please just give me a chance. Third time they did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:30.880
so now it’s direct and if there’s Netflix people&nbsp;
watching, thank you very much. I really appreciate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:34.800
that you let me have that chance. And I made&nbsp;
delivery. I made delivery two months ahead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:40.000
their delivery schedule and everything’s fine&nbsp;
and they’ve already confirmed delivery and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:45.200
it’s perfect delivery and you know their delivery&nbsp;
schedule is five pages long. I mean and by the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:51.920
I have to deliver those movies in 18 languages&nbsp;
and six subtitles, there’s 25 versions of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:59.360
and delivery alone is a lot more money than a lot&nbsp;
of indie filmmakers spend on making their movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:04.320
just making delivery. All the soundtracks, all&nbsp;
the M&amp;E tracks, everything. I mean it’s a very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:13.680
very extensive process making delivery but there’s&nbsp;
companies you use who are certified…I forget what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:19.920
you call them…not aggregators, whatever - they&nbsp;
deliver, the specialize in delivering to Netflix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:24.080
and you hire them and they do your dubs and your&nbsp;
this and your that and you just have to manage it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:29.520
all and then some stuff you do your own stuff,&nbsp;
your own artwork and things like that. Making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:35.520
delivery is a big part of the business but I&nbsp;
really appreciate that Netflix gave me the chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:44.000
to do it and I did it properly and hopefully&nbsp;
that and again that’s part of distribution.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:48.080
Film Courage: How did you get&nbsp;
the appointment to pitch? To even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:51.960
know that your pitch was too long&nbsp;
originally and shortened it down?

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:57.280
Jeff: Because at heart I’m a sales guy. I mean&nbsp;
you know remember I went from law school into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:03.280
the sales department of a distribution company.&nbsp;
That’s what you do, you get appointments and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:08.880
you meet with people and you pitch them&nbsp;
and you have to be super, super tenacious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:17.120
and very persistent and polite and the most&nbsp;
important thing is you have to - here’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:24.080
motto I tell everybody who works for me, you&nbsp;
can quote me on this - “No means not today.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:29.920
Everybody starts with a no, everybody&nbsp;
starts with a no. And if you take no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:35.520
as the definitive answer you cannot survive in&nbsp;
this business. I say no means not today. Meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:40.080
I’m not going to give you the appointment today,&nbsp;
I’m not going to give you the deal, I’m not going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:44.320
to even talk to you, I’m not going to return your&nbsp;
email. I don’t take that as personal rejection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:49.120
That’s part of the business. It’s a pain in the&nbsp;
butt but it’s part of the business and I just keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:55.120
at it over and over and over. In a very nice way&nbsp;
I just assume that it’s part of the business and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:01.600
I have 22 years of experience getting no’s.&nbsp;
There’s lots and lots of no’s. Lots of doors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:07.040
shut on your face, lots of no return phone&nbsp;
calls, thousands…tens of thousands of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:12.080
but you know ultimately if you believe&nbsp;
in your product and you know that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:17.760
have something to offer to them sooner&nbsp;
or later maybe they will give you a shot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:24.080
I used to say it takes a hundred calls&nbsp;
to get an appointment. I don’t say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:29.680
anymore. Now I say it takes 250 calls to get&nbsp;
an appointment. The number has gone up. So if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:34.800
you know that going into it and you know for all&nbsp;
the people who have worked with me in the office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:39.440
they know that that’s true, they might&nbsp;
say no Jeff you’re wrong it’s now 300.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:46.080
But you know that. That’s part of the process and&nbsp;
you just deal with it and you live with it and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:50.800
is it a little depressing and&nbsp;
aggravating, yeah of course it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:59.120
and are people totally rude and not returning&nbsp;
any emails or calls, it’s part of the process.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:02.640
Film Courage: And when you went&nbsp;
in the third time to pitch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:07.840
what are you bringing with you?&nbsp;
Just a log line? A synopsis?

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:15.680
Jeff: Okay, I’m an anomaly, independent producers&nbsp;
who are watching this do not assume. This is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:21.600
Blair Witch Project. This is not what normally&nbsp;
goes on. I had a track record from two other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:25.600
films. If I didn’t have that I wasn’t even&nbsp;
going to…every single time you do it it’s a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:32.080
adventure. So they know that I can make the movie&nbsp;
on time and on budget and on a quality movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:37.040
That helps open the door and get the&nbsp;
appointment to start with. Secondly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:44.880
everything got delivered on time and thirdly they&nbsp;
did well with the movies. Had they not done well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:50.560
I wasn’t going to get another appointment it’s&nbsp;
business. It’s all statistics, they look at their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:55.200
little iPad in front of you and they say okay&nbsp;
your numbers are…they don’t tell you the numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:02.080
(confidential). But if your numbers aren’t&nbsp;
good - one you aren’t getting an appoint and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:08.080
two you’re not getting a deal so how here’s&nbsp;
the big question, so how does a new filmmaker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:11.200
even get the first appointment? How&nbsp;
do you get the first time in there?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:16.720
Here is how I think you get it in…you’ve&nbsp;
got to go in with somebody else.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:21.520
You’ve got to go in with an established&nbsp;
producer who has a track record who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:26.240
can make believe in you or who does believe in&nbsp;
you and can hold your hand through the process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:32.000
and give you that creditability and experience&nbsp;
because not only what the streamers want and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:37.760
broadcasters but your investors, the investors&nbsp;
want. They want to know that you’re going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:41.600
able to deliver what you say you’re going can&nbsp;
deliver and if you haven’t done it before, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:46.720
doesn’t mean you can’t do it. You probably can do&nbsp;
it. It just means that they don’t know that you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:53.600
going to be and so they want to be on somebody&nbsp;
who has done it before. So for say a streamer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.560 --> 00:10:00.160
say okay Jeff will make sure your product is going&nbsp;
to get delivered on time and on budget and quality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:07.680
or an investor, I guess I have a track record that&nbsp;
helps, it really helps to have a track record and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:12.800
that’s sort of what all the agencies have. These&nbsp;
are the people you want to do business with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:15.760
people that they’ve already done business&nbsp;
with before because it’s more predicable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:21.360
So getting in the first one or two times is&nbsp;
way more difficult because you don’t have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:25.440
track record so I think the best way to do&nbsp;
that is to hook up with somebody who does.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:29.120
Unless you can get super lucky&nbsp;
and get your own appointment.

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:32.640
Film Courage: And where would one&nbsp;
meet a person of this caliber?

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:36.560
Jeff: Yes, that’s the problem. I mean generally&nbsp;
you’re going to go on the Internet, you’re going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:41.520
to watch all of these webcasts and interviews&nbsp;
and all of this kind of stuff and all of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:49.200
people who claim to be teachers and masterclasses&nbsp;
and this and that and pros and it’s tricky, it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:55.200
tricky. But you’ve kind of opened it up for me to&nbsp;
do a pitch for myself here. I mean I do consulting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.200 --> 00:11:02.400
for those people to find other people. So I don’t&nbsp;
necessarily do that for people. I have done it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:09.520
I’ve done a couple of projects where I’ve mentored&nbsp;
them, once in awhile I’ll do that but usually what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:16.560
I do as a consultant I hire myself as a consultant&nbsp;
to hook those people up with other like people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:21.680
either certain genres because by the way&nbsp;
when you’re dealing with Netflix now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:26.320
there are…there’s got to be thousands…there’s&nbsp;
got to be I don’t know five to a thousand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:32.640
buyers. There are so many departments now with&nbsp;
so many little niches. So if you fit into this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:38.080
category - if you’re horror, psychological&nbsp;
thriller, blah, blah, blah, there’s like maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:42.800
five different categories and you need to go into&nbsp;
the right one so you’ve got to be hooked up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:47.440
somebody who kind of knows how to navigate&nbsp;
that one. It’s a little tricky these days.

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:50.880
Film Courage: Okay, here’s another&nbsp;
question…so I’m at an event&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.800 --> 00:12:02.400
and maybe it’s film oriented or maybe it’s AFM,&nbsp;
whatever. How am I approaching someone…I see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:06.680
them from across the room. I realize this&nbsp;
person could get me in that door [for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.680 --> 00:12:10.160
meeting to pitch]. How do I approach them&nbsp;
where I’m not disingenuous, where I am not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:15.920
this sort of sleazy, LA opportunist…or&nbsp;
maybe all the rules go out the window?

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:21.600
Jeff: Great, great question&nbsp;
and it really boils down to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:25.920
how do you do it in a bar? I mean you&nbsp;
know like it just depends on who you are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:32.320
what mood you’re in, how many drinks you’ve&nbsp;
had…it really does. It’s a chemistry thing.

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:33.680
Film Courage: That’s fair.

00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:41.120
Jeff: It’s true, sometimes you just feel a little&nbsp;
more daring. You don’t want to be that sleazy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:50.080
LA type of thing. Nobody wants that but you know&nbsp;
sometimes people respect…like it could be I don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:54.480
know how to do it. I was never great at a bar&nbsp;
(ironically). I’m pretty good in a sales room&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.480 --> 00:13:00.320
but I’m not that good in a bar. I just don’t&nbsp;
feel comfortable in that environment. And it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:05.600
the same, standing in the lobby at the AFM and the&nbsp;
hotel there, it’s tricky, it’s really tricky. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:11.040
they’re usually talking to people and they always&nbsp;
look so busy, you don’t want to interrupt so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:16.560
that’s got nothing to do with filmmaking&nbsp;
and everything to do with interpersonal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:22.480
skills and confidence and humility&nbsp;
and whatever it’s chemistry.

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:26.240
Film Courage: I think that’s a fair answer.&nbsp;
You’re right and there are people who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:29.760
masters at it. I see them at Starbucks and&nbsp;
all over where they have this It Factor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:37.200
they can start conversations and it’s not&nbsp;
over-the-top “I need something from you.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:42.640
And there are other people and I can’t even say&nbsp;
that they’re trying to be sleazy or disingenuous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:49.867
it’s just an approach. You’re right. I don’t&nbsp;
feel comfortable in bars either so I get it.

00:13:49.867 --> 00:13:53.440
Jeff: It’s a chemistry thing so I&nbsp;
don’t know. That’s the word I use.

00:13:53.440 --> 00:13:54.800
Film Courage: That’s a good point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:59.200
and I don’t know I guess chemistry&nbsp;
can be manipulated. I don’t know?

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:06.480
Jeff: Yes and it’s also funny all of these&nbsp;
markets and festivals have these big parties.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:10.640
The problem with the parties is they’re&nbsp;
so loud. You can’t really talk to anybody,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:17.413
you can’t really engage but maybe that’s better.&nbsp;
I don’t know? It’s a social dynamic that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:25.200
tricky. I generally approach it as real business&nbsp;
like that’s who I am. Just real straight up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:30.320
business guy which isn’t…sometimes that doesn’t&nbsp;
work. A lot of people want it to be more social.

00:14:31.520 --> 00:14:35.280
Film Courage: Yes, but then sometimes the&nbsp;
ones who are really good at that there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:38.240
like a flakiness. So there’s&nbsp;
a really fine line to it.

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:42.080
Jeff: And it depends on the other&nbsp;
person right? How they receive that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.080 --> 00:14:46.480
Some people like to have that flaky&nbsp;
thing, some people don’t but you don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:50.080
know because you don’t know the person so&nbsp;
you have no idea. It’s like blind dating.

00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:54.640
Film Courage: Yes, that’s true.&nbsp;
Good point. Have you only pitched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.640 --> 00:14:58.560
Netflix three times and you were successful&nbsp;
at all pitches or there were more times?

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:04.720
Jeff: There were more times. I forgot to&nbsp;
mention that. There were many more times when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:09.280
they were not as receptive, which is&nbsp;
fair game right? You can’t like anything.

00:15:09.280 --> 00:15:11.680
Film Courage: But it taught you something?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:14.960
I know it’s a cliche but you&nbsp;
learned to shorten the pitch maybe?

00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:19.360
Jeff: No. They didn’t like it. They&nbsp;
didn’t like what I had to offer so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.360 --> 00:15:25.440
fair is fair. You can’t expect everybody to&nbsp;
like everything. I mean I respectfully disagree.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:31.040
I think the other stuff I pitched was in some&nbsp;
ways even better but it didn’t happen that time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:35.600
But my motto is “No means not today,”&nbsp;
so those are on the back burner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:39.680
There will be another cycle. I will&nbsp;
pitch them again. I have to wait for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:45.840
new people to come in or you know to change&nbsp;
departments or whatever and then do it again.

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:51.520
Film Courage: Are you bringing in art, key&nbsp;
art as well or no, that doesn’t factor in?

00:15:51.520 --> 00:15:59.360
Jeff: Oh yeah, especially at a streaming company,&nbsp;
yes. And at a distributor you have to because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:04.000
distributor wants to make the key art so&nbsp;
they actually don’t want you…they want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:07.840
do it unless you’ve had it done professionally&nbsp;
really good and you’re a good artist (whatever).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:16.720
I always bring in key art. It gives a visual&nbsp;
image and I bring in a trailer and key art and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:22.240
a log line, that’s it, that’s all you&nbsp;
need. It’s pretty short and sweet.

00:16:22.760 --> 00:16:27.440
Film Courage: So then how does it go?&nbsp;
“Okay great,” they tell you right there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.440 --> 00:16:28.757
“No we can’t do this.” Or&nbsp;
they say “We’ll let you know.”

00:16:28.757 --> 00:16:29.920
Jeff: Yes, they say “We’ll let you know.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:39.840
But after you leave those meetings you analyze&nbsp;
that “We’ll let you know for a long time.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:44.240
Was it a good “We’ll let you know?”&nbsp;
Was it a medium one? A bad one?

00:16:44.240 --> 00:16:45.375
Film Courage: Was there an inflection at the end?

00:16:45.375 --> 00:16:46.400
Jeff: Was there an inflection.

00:16:46.400 --> 00:16:47.920
Film Courage: Did they raise an eyebrow?

00:16:47.920 --> 00:16:48.800
Jeff: Was it on the left?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:59.360
You drive yourself crazy. You just hope that&nbsp;
on your follow-up you get a good result.

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:05.440
Film Courage: What’s your&nbsp;
time frame for following up?

00:17:05.440 --> 00:17:08.080
Jeff: I usually ask them when I should follow-up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.360 --> 00:17:12.720
Actually I used to ask now I say “I’ll&nbsp;
follow up with you in a week. Is that okay?”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:20.320
Because you don’t want to appear that you’re&nbsp;
bugging them or pestering them or whatever and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.320 --> 00:17:25.520
you do and you’ve got to be good at follow up and&nbsp;
then hopefully get a response. If you don’t you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:30.000
politely follow up you know a couple days later.&nbsp;
Everything has got to be polite and professional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:35.760
even though they’re not necessarily…I’m not saying&nbsp;
Netflix. Netflix is actually the most professional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:42.080
I’ve ever done and I’m not saying that for the&nbsp;
video, it’s true. Their follow-up is unbelievable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.080 --> 00:17:47.280
they return calls. For me anyways. I’ve been lucky&nbsp;
that my buyer has been super, super responsive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:52.560
polite and professional. Whereas some people you&nbsp;
will never hear anything from them (distributors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.560 --> 00:17:58.000
primarily). You wonder if your email is even&nbsp;
working. You didn’t get a bounce back but you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:04.640
often wonder. So you follow up politely. You be&nbsp;
as professional as possible and you know just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.640 --> 00:18:22.560
sometimes it works and sometimes it doesn’t.&nbsp;
Questions for the viewer: What was helpful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.560 --> 00:18:23.913
about this video? Anything not helpful?

